<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">American Samoa</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – present · capital Pago Pago</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Samoan islands ceded to the United States from 1900, an unincorporated territory where Samoan custom and communal land endure.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a> ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a> and gave the islands east of longitude 171 west to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, which prized the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a> as a coaling station. Chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aunuu">Aunuʻu</a> signed a deed of cession in 1900, and the rulers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-manu-a">Kingdom of Manuʻa</a> ceded their islands in 1904.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> governed the territory until 1951 through naval commandants, leaving village affairs and land to the matai, the chiefs of extended families. A local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau movement</a> in the 1920s protested naval rule. Authority passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Interior in 1951, and a constitution adopted in 1960 was revised in 1967. Tuna canning became the main industry.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>American Samoans elected their first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-tali-coleman">Peter Tali Coleman</a>, in 1977. The territory remains unincorporated and unorganised; its people are US nationals rather than citizens, an arrangement many defend as protecting communal land and the faʻa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a> way of life.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/american-samoa.jpg" alt="American Samoa" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: by Tobias Jakobs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_American_Samoa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pago Pago</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Tali Coleman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism, Latter-day Saint movement</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Samoan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Samoa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1963&amp;focus=american-samoa" aria-label="American Samoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8 2709 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where American Samoa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M328 2965L294 2948M328 2965L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M294 2948h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M328 2965h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M328 2965h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pago Pago. <a href="/atlas?year=1963&amp;focus=american-samoa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
